Say where an extension message goes, and where it stops.

On a phone Tampermonkey's content script calls chrome.runtime.sendMessage
and nothing happens: no reply, no runtime.lastError, and neither onMessage
nor onConnect fires in the service worker. The same call from the
extension's own popup is answered. Scripts cannot be installed, because
the content script is what tells the background to start.

Every refusal on the browser's side is silent in the page. The checks on
an incoming channel report a bad message, which would end the renderer --
on a single-process phone that is the browser, so it is only logged -- and
the later ones return without telling the opener at all. So on OHOS, at
ERROR:

  - each incoming channel, with its endpoint type, extension, target,
    source URL and process, and which check refused it if one did;
  - each place the message service drops a channel without telling the
    opener: the source gone, the opener's port invalid, no receiver
    (including none left once the opener's own frames are removed);
  - waiting for the background to start, whether it did, and the channel
    opening.

The next run showed the channel opening and reaching the service worker
-- as a user script's, through onUserScriptMessage, which Tampermonkey
does not wait on. The renderer decides that from the world the script
runs in: the isolated world for manifest and scripting.executeScript
content scripts, the user script world for chrome.userScripts. So the
renderer also says, for each extension world a context is created in,
which world it is, whose, how it was classified and on what page -- which
shows whether the manifest content script was injected at all.

Refusals are always logged; channels that go the ordinary way, the first
twenty and then every hundredth.

Diagnostic; it comes out once content-script messaging works on a phone.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch.

